From a06563587cfadd10954258a8323c4818a0642cc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BHARATH CHANDRA VADDARAM <bharath.vbcr@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:20:23 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Carry the written flag across a closure boundary by
 replaying the closure's effects in its creator

A write and the Close that follows it only met when both sat in one function: a closure and its
creator are lowered separately, so a closure that wrote what its creator closed (and the reverse),
a Close in a closure nested one level in, and the body of a range-over-func against the function
around it all missed ERR005.

Each closure now gets a summary (closureeffects.go): the captured variables it may write, through
the closures it makes and runs as well, and the Close calls whose error it drops. The creator
replays the summary on its own slots for those variables where the closure runs: a write at every
use of the closure (a call, a go statement, an escape), a close at a call, a defer or a call that
takes the closure as an argument (a range-over-func body). A closure that is returned, stored or
passed on and closes a captured resource cannot see its creator's flow, so its entry is seeded
writable when the creator side may write that variable anywhere; the answer comes from a
per-creator index, built once. A closure that stores to a captured variable contributes no
writes, since they may be to the resource it replaces. The summary and the syntax scan are cached
per function, and the work is counted in planWork and bounded by a linearity test.

Twelve fixture markers pinned the miss and flip in this commit, each with a ft-c7dd comment:
advfv3b3c/a.go P1b, P3a, P3b, P3b2, P3c and P4e2, advfvrange3b3c/r.go P5a, P5c, P5e and P5g,
advsameline/a.go closureClose and errrangecapture/rc.go writtenAfterLoop (whose comment, which
said the shape is a documented miss, is updated). tests/stress/testdata/src/er_w_rangecapture
main.go:40 carries the same writtenAfterLoop shape as a MISS marker; it is held by another lane and
needs promoting to HIT.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
